### Rust ###
# Generated by Cargo
# will have compiled files and executables
debug/
target/

# libFuzzer's crash reproducers and the inputs it grows, from the detached
# workspace in `fuzz/`. The named seeds in `fuzz/corpus/session/` are committed;
# everything a run adds there is a fact about that run. `target/` above already
# covers `fuzz/target`.
/fuzz/artifacts/
/fuzz/corpus/session/*
!/fuzz/corpus/session/empty
!/fuzz/corpus/session/envelope-empty
!/fuzz/corpus/session/snapshot-empty
!/fuzz/corpus/session/snapshot-valid

# `Cargo.lock` is deliberately NOT ignored. This is a binary, so the lockfile is
# what makes a build reproducible, and one pin in it is load-bearing: grammers
# 0.10.0 does not compile against glass_pumpkin 2.0.0-rc1, so the lockfile holds
# 2.0.0-rc0 on purpose. A `cargo update` drops it. See AGENTS.md.
#
# Remove Cargo.lock from gitignore if creating an executable, leave it for libraries
# More information here https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/guide/cargo-toml-vs-cargo-lock.html
# Cargo.lock

# These are backup files generated by rustfmt
**/*.rs.bk

# MSVC Windows builds of rustc generate these, which store debugging information
*.pdb

.DS_Store

# Local configuration and the log the client writes beside it, under the same
# name with a `.log` extension: run artifacts, never source.
televim.toml
*.log

# Python bytecode, from the measurement drivers under scripts/memory/ being
# imported rather than only run. A run artifact, never source.
__pycache__/

# ai
.workbuddy-ai/
.opencode/index

// env
.env

# The local OpenCode skills the workflow runs from: tooling that ships with the
# development workflow, not with the client.
.agents/
